@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Testing strategy — four layers, speed traded for fidelity

' purpose: the four test layers, and the failure modes the def-level bash suites
'          have actually exhibited — every one of them a test that passed
' covers: internal/contract/**
' covers: e2e/**
' covers: internal/tmux/**
' covers: defs/tests/**
' covers: defs/*/test.sh
' covers: defs/*/tests/*.sh
' covers: defs/sidecars/mitmproxy/test.sh
' covers: e2e/preconditions_test.go (skipOutsideSbx)
'   the suite's own standards doc ... e2e/go-testing-standards.md
'
' Image resolution for these suites (proveo_test_image in defs/lib/test-image.sh,
' and why :latest could only
' ever mean "whatever the registry last published") is
' _spec/_devops/image-lineage-and-publish.puml.
'
' ── FOUR WAYS A def SUITE REPORTED PASS WHILE TESTING NOTHING ───────────────
' Recorded because each cost real time, and because all four are shapes rather
' than one-off bugs.
'
' 1. A SUITE THAT REBUILT WHAT IT TESTED. claudecode's phase 1 used to BUILD the
'    image it then asserted against, with a bare `docker build` and no BASE_IMAGE
'    build-arg — so `ARG BASE_IMAGE=proveo/base-node-lsp:latest`, the Dockerfile's
'    default, applied. That is the PUBLISHED base. Every run therefore threw away
'    the local lineage that the parent ensure step (then build.sh and ensure.sh,
'    now imagebuild.BuildTarget) exists to establish, overwrote
'    the operator's image with a registry-based one, and then asserted against the
'    result.
'    It surfaced as `bun: command not found`: bun was added to proveo/base-node
'    AFTER the last base-node-lsp publish, so a locally built claudecode had it
'    and the one this phase substituted did not. Rebuilding by hand "fixed" it
'    exactly until the next run of the suite clobbered it again — twice, before
'    the mechanism was found.
'    opencode and cursor verify AVAILABILITY here and never build; claudecode now
'    matches them. Building is imagebuild's job (internal/imagebuild/targets.go),
'    and it is the only caller that knows how to resolve the base chain:
'    `proveo build claudecode`.
'
' 2. A MISSING HELPER THAT STILL PRODUCED A NUMBER. test_config.sh had called
'    `run_timeout` since it was written and claudecode's helpers never defined it
'    (opencode's and cursor's both do). It failed as `run_timeout: command not
'    found`, and because the caller is
'      BAD_RULES=$(run_timeout ... docker run ... | grep -c 'mcp__\*' || true)
'    the empty pipeline still produced "0" — so the wildcard-MCP-rule assertion
'    reported PASS without ever starting a container. A test that cannot fail.
'
' 3. TWO NEGATIVES OVER A PATH THAT NO LONGER EXISTED. In test_config.sh both
'    tool-permission checks are `assert_failure`. While they pointed at the
'    retired /opt/claudecode/defaults/agents, `grep` found no directory, failed,
'    and both reported PASS having verified nothing. A POSITIVE assertion now goes
'    first, so a missing tree fails loudly instead of turning the two negatives
'    vacuous again.
'
' 4. A `set -e` CASCADE THAT HID NINE PHASES. test_config.sh sed'd
'    `seed_claude_subagents` out of /entrypoint.sh, but the renderer had already
'    become `render_subagents` in the SHARED lib, called from proveo_seed's
'    per-harness case. The old spelling matched nothing, so `eval ""` succeeded
'    and the call died `command not found` (127) — which under `set -e` took the
'    whole suite down after that phase. Every phase from Workspace onward never
'    ran.
'
' ── AND ONE THAT REPORTED FAIL WHILE TESTING FINE ──────────────────────────
' The inverse shape, and it reads as a broken product rather than a broken test.
'
' A ROOT CONTAINER WRITING INTO A ""t.TempDir()"". e2e/scope_mounts_test.go probes
' the provisioning paths with ""docker run --user root"" and the temp dir bind-
' mounted at /w. Root is DELIBERATE — those paths install packages — but anything
' the install leaves behind is owned by root, and Go's TempDir cleanup runs as the
' TEST USER. MEASURED: the absent-node_modules subtest supplies no node_modules,
' so ""ensure_dependency_trees"" auto-installs, and ""pnpm install"" left
' /w/.pnpm-store and /w/node_modules as root:root —
'   TempDir RemoveAll cleanup: unlinkat …/.pnpm-store/v10/files/7b: permission denied
' on a subtest whose own assertion had already PASSED. The signal points at the
' code under test; the cause is the probe's own leftovers.
'
' Turning auto-install off is not the fix: ""_proveo_auto_install_enabled || return 0""
' is the FIRST line of ensure_dependency_trees, so disabling it silences the very
' warning the subtest asserts.
'
' So ONE helper — ""rootLibProbe"" — owns the invocation and hands ownership back
' with a ""trap … EXIT"", which fires on the ""exit 1""s the probe scripts use to
' report failure too. Without that, the first REAL failure here would be followed
' by a cleanup failure masking it. The file's other probes already avoided this by
' running as ""--user hostUIDGID(t)""; the four root ones now clean up instead.
'
' ── THE ROSTER IS READ FROM THE IMAGE, NEVER RESTATED ───────────────────────
' Subagents are no longer under /opt/<harness>/defaults/agents. They are one
' shared tree at /opt/proveo/subagents with a per-harness _roster.json, and
' cursor's and opencode's test_defaults.sh went on asserting the retired path —
' 10 failures against an image that was built correctly, which reads as a broken
' image rather than a stale test.
'
' So the roster is READ FROM THE IMAGE. A list copied into a test drifts silently
' the moment the roster changes; cecli's test.sh already took this approach
' ("read from the image, never restated here") and did not go stale.
' `tools:` and `readonly: true` moved with them, into the per-harness FRONTMATTER
' (_frontmatter/<harness>/<agent>.yaml) that render_subagents applies to the
' shared body — the bodies never carried either, so they are asserted there.
' See _spec/defs/agent-definition-sharing.puml.
'
' ── WHAT A def SUITE IS ALLOWED TO ASSERT ───────────────────────────────────
' These suites assert what the IMAGE carries. When cursor's test_tools.sh was
' INVERTED on 2026-09-05 to expect NO docker binaries, that was correct for
' exactly this reason: the image now deliberately carries none. Whether a sandbox
' can still REACH a daemon is a different question, owned by e2e/sbx_test.go's
' assertSandboxSuppliesDocker, which was deliberately not inverted.
'
' ── WHEN A def SUITE MAY SKIP RATHER THAN ASSERT ────────────────────────────
' Only when the property is IMPOSSIBLE on the host running the suite, never when
' it is merely inconvenient — and the reason must be printed, so a skip reads as
' a fact about the machine rather than a hole in the coverage.
'
' The case that set this: `[sol] solc is installed` failed on every arm64 run
' with `OSError: [Errno 8] Exec format error` on solc-0.8.20, beside solc-select's
' own `.arm64_warning_shown` marker. solc publishes NO linux-arm64 binary, so
' solc-select installs the x86-64 one and exec cannot succeed. No Dockerfile
' change can cause or fix that, and the failure made `mise run test-images` exit
' non-zero — which ABORTS the run before cursor's and opencode's suites, so one
' impossible assertion was hiding two whole suites.
'
' Scoped as narrowly as the fact allows: only the EXEC is skipped, only on
' aarch64/arm64. `solc-select versions` still asserts the version is installed,
' solc's ABSENCE from the base variants is still asserted, and on x86-64 the
' original assertion runs unchanged. The skip prints
' "solc publishes no linux-arm64 build; solc-select installs the x86-64 one".
'
' A note on the judgement, because this is a test edited to make a suite pass:
' the honest test of that move is whether the assertion could EVER hold here. It
' cannot. Had it been failing for a reason the tree controls, the fix would have
' been the tree.
'
' Two smaller conventions worth keeping:
'   * the baked uid/gid is asserted against the Dockerfile's `ARG USER_ID` (1000,
'     named "claude" in claudecode), overridable via EXPECTED_UID;
'   * NODE_ENV is asserted ABSENT, because baking it would make the agent's own
'     `npm`/`pnpm install` skip devDependencies — the project's .env is the only
'     place allowed to set it.
' The Claude-in-Chrome phase runs a FAKE host relay inside the container in place
' of `proveo run`'s, so the chain is driven end to end without a browser
' (_spec/defs/claudecode/chrome-bridge.puml).
'
' ── THE GO SUITES SKIP FOR ONE REASON: A MISSING PREREQUISITE ───────────────
' The `//go:build e2e` tag IS the opt-in. A suite that compiles under it must
' decide for ITSELF whether this host can run it, so the only reason to skip is a
' missing PREREQUISITE — PTY driver, container backend, harness image, credential
' — never an unset ceremonial flag. A green run with every test skipped is then an
' honest statement about the host, and one that `go test -v` prints the reason for.
'
' The floor, and why each check is shaped the way it is:
'   * requireTmux — tmux is the PTY driver every harness run needs.
'   * requireDocker — checks the CLI on PATH, deliberately NOT the daemon: every
'     suite here shells out to `docker`, and the endpoint (context or DOCKER_HOST)
'     is resolved per-run by dockerHost.
'   * harnessImage — building is a maintainer action (`mise run build <target>`),
'     so a missing image is a skip, not a failure.
'   * requireHarnessCredential — the credential VARIABLES come from the def's own
'     manifest (`env[].secret`, in declaration order), never restated, so a harness
'     that changes what it authenticates with cannot drift out of the guard. It
'     resolves through hostEnvValue rather than os.Getenv, because this suite
'     accepts a key from the process environment OR the repo .env, and checking
'     only the first skipped runs the developer had in fact provisioned.
'   * requireReviewTier — mirrors reviewSupported in cmd/proveo, which is the
'     source of truth: linux only, and a unix:// DOCKER_HOST. `proveo run` already
'     warns on an unsupported host that "the consent gate cannot be reached from
'     the inspector on this host, so every new connection will be DENIED without a
'     prompt". Without the skip the test does not fail fast, it TIMES OUT waiting
'     for an overlay that will never render — 90 seconds spent to report a host
'     capability as a defect.
'
' ── :local BEFORE :latest, WHEREVER A SUITE RESOLVES AN IMAGE ───────────────
' `mise run build` tags a local build :local and only a publish moves :latest, so
' harnessImageName prefers :local and falls back to :latest (overridable per target
' with PROVEO_TEST_IMAGE_<TARGET>). Defaulting to :latest skipped these suites on a
' machine that had just built the thing under test — and on one that had also
' pulled, ran the PUBLISHED image while the report read as though it covered the
' local build. anyImagePresent applies the same rule to guards and teardown: a
' guard naming one tag skipped the entire suite whenever the developer had built
' the other, and a cleanup naming one removed nothing.
'
' ── A FIFTH VACUOUS SHAPE: ZERO OF N, REPORTED AS ALL OF N ──────────────────
' The four above are bash. This one is Go, and the same shape. The two backends
' keep a secret out of the launch plan by different means: docker declares the
' `proveo-brokered` sentinel in argv, while sbx puts nothing there and hands
' values to `sbx secret set` on stdin. Read blind, an sbx plan satisfied the
' sentinel loop by containing no keys to check — zero of N, reported as "all
' forwarded". assertPlanIsolation now branches on isSbxArgv: on the docker branch
' `declared == 0` is itself an error, and on the sandbox branch ANY provider key in
' argv is an error, because argv is visible to anyone listing processes.
'
' ── AN ASSERTION MUST EXPLAIN ITS OWN FAILURE ───────────────────────────────
' e2e/instrument_test.go is the shared instrumentation for the tmux-driven layer,
' and every assertion that can time out fails through it, because the failures that
' cost the most to diagnose are the ones that report nothing useful. Three rules,
' each learned from a failure that hid its own cause:
'   1. Snapshot the scrollback WHILE the session is alive. Capturing only at the
'      moment of failure returns "no server running" once the run has exited, which
'      hides whatever it printed on the way out.
'   2. A single empty capture is not death. tmux returns nothing mid-redraw and
'      during long output (an image pull), so only CONSECUTIVE misses count —
'      deadAfter = 4.
'   3. Always include exited containers (`docker ps -a`). "Started and died" and
'      "never started" are different faults and look identical without it.
'   4. Read proveo's OWN run log, and read it here. A session that dies early
'      takes its PROVEO_HOME with it, because that home is a `t.TempDir()` the
'      framework removes when the test ends — so the one artifact that says WHY
'      is gone before anyone can open it, and the pane only carries what fit on
'      screen before the process exited. `runLogTail` scrapes the path out of
'      the run's own header line and tails the file while the directory still
'      exists. That capture is the difference between "session exited before
'      the agent shell" and a cause: it named the next two failures on the run
'      it was added for, after three wrong guesses had been made without it.
'      What the log holds, and why it is the record that outlives the terminal,
'      is _spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript.puml.
'
' ── EITHER DELIVERY ROUTE COUNTS, AND THE SECOND ONE ARRIVES LATE ──────────
' Three tests have now learned the same lesson, so it is stated once here. The
' two renderings put the agent's work in different places: DOCKER advances the
' mounted worktree the test can stat, while the SANDBOX clones by default, so
' the agent writes and commits inside the clone and TEARDOWN fetches those
' commits into refs/proveo/<sid>/ — the mounted directory never gains the file
' (_spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml). A probe that checks only the mount
' is asserting a docker fact, and pinning the backend to make it true again is
' banned by the section below. The worktree case and the `-egress` container
' cases were the first two; hello_world_test.go was the third, and reported "no
' hello-world file on the host" for a run whose own transcript said
' `Commit 261c544 feat: add HELLO_WORLD.txt`.
'
' Two follow-on faults are the part worth writing down, because accepting both
' routes is not enough on its own:
'   * THE FETCH LAGS THE PANE. The tmux session can disappear before teardown
'     has finished writing the refs, so a single observation taken the moment
'     the pane dies reads as "delivered by no route" on a run that then printed
'     the refs it had written. The assertion polls a bounded while longer
'     (PROVEO_TEST_TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT, 2 minutes) instead of asking once.
'   * THE NAME AND THE BODY COME FROM ONE PLACE. Reading the file name from
'     the refs and then the body off disk sets "found" for a file the host does
'     not hold, and the marker assertion fails against an empty body — a
'     delivery success reported as a corrupt deliverable. Whichever route
'     answers returns both values together.
'
' ── A LAYER-4 CASE LAUNCHES THE PRODUCT, NOT A REPLICA OF IT ───────────────
' Seven cases used to reassemble by hand what `proveo run` assembles itself: a
' `sbx create -p <host>:9222` plus a manually started CDP relay and a manual
' `sbx exec … agent-browser open`; a `docker run --entrypoint bash <img> -c
' script` carrying the mounts, the workdir and the uid copied out of the run
' path; a `sbx create --clone`. Each was a SECOND implementation of the thing
' under test, and a second implementation drifts — the replica keeps reporting
' success after the assembly it imitated has moved. Driving `proveo run
' <target>` under tmux instead exercises choiceui, add-on selection and the
' whole entrypoint chain as shipped.
'
' The conversion also DELETED work the scripts used to do for themselves. The
' old bodies began `source /entrypoint-lib.sh; bridge_git_identity "$PWD"` or
' `ensure_git_safe_directory "$PWD"; scope_git_worktree "$PWD"`, which is the
' replica in miniature: the real entrypoint had already run all three before
' the prompt appeared, so the manual call proved only that the function could
' be invoked, never that the run invokes it.
'
' TWO SHARED HELPERS CARRY IT, and each earns its shape.
'   launchShell — one `proveo run <target> --shell` under tmux, pinned to
'     `--egress-mode open --credentials forward` with PROVEO_WIZARD=off and
'     PROVEO_MOUNT_GH_CONFIG=0, returning only once the agent's own prompt
'     appears. The posture is FIXED so a case asserting on workspace or git
'     behaviour is not also asserting on egress. The BACKEND is not pinned —
'     see the ban below — so the prompt it waits for is whichever backend the
'     host resolves.
'   shellExec — the script runs as a CHILD `bash -c` of the already-live
'     interactive shell rather than as the shell itself, so an `exit 1` inside
'     a probe reports a status instead of ending the session. It returns the
'     pane plus that status, so bodies written for `docker run … -c script`
'     keep their `strings.Contains` assertions unchanged.
'
' A SEEDED CHOICE, NEVER A DRIVEN CHECKBOX. Where a case needs an add-on on
' ("browser", "claude-in-chrome"), it writes an agentsettings.Store into an
' ISOLATED proveo home and lets the real choiceui form come up with the box
' already ticked; acceptChoicePrompt then confirms it with one Enter, the same
' pattern every other seeded-choice case uses. Navigating the picker's
' checkboxes would make each of those cases a test of the form's key bindings
' as well, and those have moved before.
'
' AND TWO CASES THAT MAY NOT CONVERT, named here so nobody converts them later:
'   TestGitRunsWhenWorktreeOwnerDiffersFromRunAsUID mounts ONLY `.git` at
'     /app/.git — no working tree, no real workspace — to reproduce git's
'     "dubious ownership" abort under a uid mismatch. A real run always mounts a
'     full workspace and never constructs that partial shape, so there is no
'     launch to convert it into.
'   TestClaudecodeEntrypointOperatesOnTheInputDir depends on PROVEO_SMOKE_TEST,
'     a self-check built into entrypoint.sh that no `proveo run` flag reaches.
' The rule the pair draws: a replica is legitimate exactly when it constructs a
' shape the product cannot, and illegitimate when it merely re-spells one the
' product already builds.
'
' Both are now SKIPPED as well, and the two rules stack rather than collide. They
' may not convert, because no launch builds their shape; and they may not run,
' because the shape they build by hand is a `docker run`. What survives is the
' record of why each exists, which is the only part a reader ever needed — the
' day either question comes back, it comes back on sbx.
'
' ── A PANE IS AN ECHO CHAMBER: NEVER WAIT ON TEXT YOU TYPED ────────────────
' Measured 2026-09-15. shellExec typed `bash -c '<script>'; echo <marker>=$?` and
' then waited for `<marker>=` as a plain substring. The pane shows INPUT as well
' as output, so the wait was satisfied by the terminal's echo of the command —
' the same instant it was sent, before bash ran a line of the script. The screen
' it returned held only the typed line, whose trailing `=$?` parsed as no status,
' and the -1 fallback dressed that up as an answer. Every tmux-driven test in the
' suite failed at once, each blaming the thing it was probing: git unwritable in
' all five harnesses, no scope mode usable, no worktree usable, docker access
' missing everywhere. One broken probe, thirty-odd false verdicts, and nothing in
' the output pointing at the probe.
'
' Three rules, and the second is why this took a whole suite to notice:
'   5. A completion marker must be UNSPELLABLE by the input that requests it.
'      `echo '<head>''<tail>'=$?` keeps the quote characters between the halves on
'      the typed line and leaves the shell's echo the only place the marker
'      appears whole. clone_output_test.go already knew this — it matches its
'      probe's answer by shape at column 0 "because the shell echoes the command
'      first" — so the knowledge existed and the shared helper did not have it.
'   6. A probe that cannot read its own answer must FAIL, not return a sentinel.
'      `status := -1` made a broken probe indistinguishable from a command that
'      ran and failed. markerStatus returns (int, bool) and shellExec fatals on
'      false, so the next time this breaks it says so in one line.
'   7. A FIXED SLEEP IS A TIMING ASSUMPTION ABOUT ONE BACKEND, so wait on the
'      marker instead. TestAgentSettingsPersistAcrossRuns slept 4s for the shell
'      and 2s for the answer; both were long enough on the docker rendering and
'      neither was on the sandbox one, so the pane was captured holding the
'      typed line and no answer. An empty tier read as a cache that had failed
'      to re-enter — the test accused the mechanism it exists to protect. It
'      now waits through waitForContainerShell and shellExec, which return when
'      the agent has actually answered rather than when a clock says it should
'      have.
'
' A credential the AGENT cannot spend is a condition of the host, not a defect, so
' the watcher's Fatalf turns a pane matching credentialUnavailable into a SKIP with
' the diagnostics attached. That pattern is deliberately narrow — `credit balance
' is too low`, `insufficient credits|quota|balance`, `authentication_error`,
' `invalid x-api-key`, `invalid api key` — because broadening it to any early exit
' would let a real adapter defect leave as a skip.
'
' Two waits carry the same reasoning. waitForContainerShell treats the agent
' prompt as the signal that the WHOLE topology came up — sidecars, networks, CA
' trust — because proveo only hands the PTY over once the container is running, so
' anything failing earlier fails HERE with the scrollback attached rather than as a
' confusing timeout further down. It matches the PROMPT'S SHAPE, `…@host:workdir`
' followed by `$` or `#` at end of line, never a literal workdir, and never "$ ":
' tmux trims trailing whitespace, so a space-suffixed match never fires.
'
' Matching a literal ":/app$" was the whole reason seven tests read as hung.
' Measured 2026-09-15 across one run, both backends draw a prompt and neither is
' wrong:
'
'   docker   `I have no name!@8101a68d15b5:/app$`
'   sandbox  `claude@proveo-1789458382-46514:003$`
'            `opencode@proveo-1789459221-51842:001$`
'
' sbx runs the shell with the workspace at its own host path, so bash renders the
' BASENAME — `001`, `003` — and a matcher looking for the string "/app" cannot
' fire however long it waits. The prompts were on screen the entire time; four
' OpenCodeAuth legs each burned a 4-minute deadline staring at one.
' acceptChoicePrompt anchors on the key hint
' ("enter accept", 90s) rather than the form's title: the title is copy, it has been
' reworded once, and that reword broke a test that waited on it.
'
' ── TEARDOWN IS SHARED STATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' `proveo clean --force` removes EVERY proveo container, so running it after each
' test can tear down a sibling still coming up. forceCleanIfCrowded therefore only
' sweeps once at least cleanThreshold = 10 containers carrying the
' `proveo.egress.session` label have accumulated; below that the risk outweighs the
' benefit and the suite-level clean handles it.
'
' ── AND SANDBOXES ARE SHARED STATE NEITHER KEY SWEEPS ──────────────────────
' Measured 2026-09-15. This is the INTERACTION of two decisions, neither wrong on
' its own. A sandbox is named for its def AND its workspace
' (_spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml) so that a second run re-attaches
' instead of building another VM — and this suite hands every test its own
' `t.TempDir()` workspace. Def+workspace keying therefore yields NO reuse here:
' each test still builds a VM of its own, and the saving the key exists for is
' exactly the saving a suite cannot collect.
'
' sbx sizes an undeclared sandbox at half the HOST rather than half of what is
' left (the Memory note in sandbox-backend.puml), so a handful alive at once
' oversubscribes the machine. A 48 GiB host went OOM, the kernel killed the test
' binary 108 assertions in, and 19 sandboxes survived the process that made them.
' The suite reported a truncated run, not a resource fault.
'
' `sweepSandboxesAfter` closes it, hooked into `harnessImage` so every case that
' resolves a harness gets it. Three properties, each deliberate:
'   * it removes only names ABSENT when the test started, so an operator's own
'     sandboxes and a sibling test's are left alone;
'   * it is registered BEFORE the tmux session's Kill, because cleanups run LIFO
'     and the session has to die before the VM underneath it;
'   * its failures are IGNORED, since proveo's own teardown already removes most
'     of these and a sweep that reddens a passing test is worse than the leak it
'     was added to prevent.
'
' ── THE ENVIRONMENT A SUITE HANDS ITS CHILD ─────────────────────────────────
' Three traps, all of which present as "the session started and printed nothing":
'   * env(1) on BSD/macOS requires every -u option BEFORE any NAME=value operand,
'     so the -u list has to come first or env tries to exec "-u" as the utility and
'     the session dies with no output.
'   * Overriding HOME hides ~/.docker and with it the active context, so the CLI
'     silently falls back to /var/run/docker.sock and every docker call — starting
'     with the image pull — hangs. Pin the endpoint explicitly: dockerHost reads
'     $DOCKER_HOST, else `docker context inspect`.
'   * ISOLATE PROVEO_HOME, NOT HOME, when what the test needs is proveo's own
'     settings out of the way. HOME is where every OTHER tool on the host keeps
'     its state as well, and sbx is one of them: overriding it moves sbx's own
'     state directory, and the run then dies at `sbx template load` against a
'     store it cannot see — reported, like every early death on this backend, as
'     "session exited before the agent shell". Measured 2026-09-15 on
'     TestAgentSettingsPersistAcrossRuns, which had isolated HOME since it was
'     written and only broke when the sandbox backend became the default. The
'     docker-endpoint trap above is the same shape, and PROVEO_HOME avoids it
'     too. What still needs a whole isolated HOME — a harness CLI's own config —
'     must pin DOCKER_HOST alongside it.
'   * An isolated proveo home is also what makes driving the choice prompt
'     deterministic. A shared one would leak into the developer's real settings
'     AND skip the prompt on every later run.
' ── sbx IS THE RUNTIME UNDER TEST; NOTHING ELSE IS A SUBJECT ───────────────
' THE GOVERNING RULE, and it decides every question below it: anything mitm or
' egress outside sbx is neither tested nor a factor, and any test that does not
' test sbx is SKIPPED. Decided 2026-09-15, and a decision about scope rather
' than a finding about a defect — the docker rendering is not broken, it has
' stopped being something this suite makes claims about.
'
' What still EXISTS in the product and is no longer a test subject: the
' mitmproxy egress sidecar and its broker.env, the `-egress` container, the
' review-tier consent gate, raw `docker run` replicas of a launch, and the
' docker+egress fallback itself.
'
' THEIR DIAGRAMS ARE NOW RETIRED TOO, and that is a second decision rather than a
' consequence of this one. Dated 2026-09-16: "sbx deprecated these egress
' mechanisms, retire them." This block used to end "the product statements about
' those live in _spec/internal/egress/** and _spec/internal/egresspolicy/**, and
' they remain true", and that sentence was the over-inference — a reader landing
' on egress-tiers.puml could not tell that the tier it describes is no longer the
' rendering proveo tests or leads with, and egress-tiers listed e2e/review_tier
' _test.go as its coverage for a case that now always skips. So the six diagrams
' of the docker mechanism were deleted rather than left standing with no proof:
'   _spec/internal/egress/egress-tiers.puml · teardown-and-signals.puml
'   _spec/internal/egresspolicy/** (four files)
'   _spec/internal/broker/off-route-stripping.puml
'   _spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy.puml · transport-portability.puml
' The code they documented still ships. A diagram retired here is retired because
' its MECHANISM is deprecated, not because it was a plan that shipped.
'
' WHAT DID NOT DIE WITH THEM, and where it went:
'   the tier vocabulary open|allowlist|review, which the CLI still accepts
'                       . _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
'   the credential boundary — inject on route, strip off it, sentinel in the
'   container . _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml
'   who brokers on sbx . _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the PTY mechanics, which internal/backend/sandbox still uses on every
'   interactive sbx run . _spec/internal/ptyproxy/pty-ownership.puml
' Only the docker-mechanism story — the Squid hop, the DLP layers, the CONNECT
' consent gate, the sidecar teardown order — went with the files.
'
' WHAT THE RULE TOOK, named so nobody restores one by accident:
'   the -egress sidecar . CredentialForwardingIntegrity/egress_broker_receives
'                         _all_keys · ProjectDotEnvAtEgressLayer
'   a live container .... ProveoHomePersistence/live_shell_roundtrip and
'                         /live_resume_roundtrip
'   sidecar teardown .... SIGINTTearsDownEgressSidecars
'   the consent gate .... ReviewTierConsentGate
'   the docker bridge ... ChromeBridgeCarriesTheHostSocketIntoTheContainer
'   the fallback itself . SandboxBackendFallsBackToDockerWhenSbxAbsent
'   raw docker replicas . GitRunsWhenWorktreeOwnerDiffersFromRunAsUID ·
'                         ClaudecodeEntrypointOperatesOnTheInputDir ·
'                         toolchain's runLib · ToolchainLibIsCurrentInEveryHarness
'
' AND ONE THAT STAYS, because the distinction is the rule's edge:
' TestCursorEgressException asserts a CLI REFUSAL CONTRACT through `--print` —
' that an unsupported axis is declined naming what IS allowed. That is a claim
' about the command surface, which both renderings share, and it costs no
' container. A test is a docker subject when the RENDERING is what it reads,
' not when the word "egress" appears in it.
'
' SKIPPED, NOT DELETED, and the distinction carries the whole rule. A skipped
' case keeps the record of what it measured and why, so the day sbx cannot
' answer a question the docker lane once answered, the probe is still written.
' A deleted one takes that with it. `skipOutsideSbx(t, subject)` is the first
' line of each, and it names the subject in the skip text, so `go test -v`
' prints a scope decision rather than an unexplained gap — the same standard
' the prerequisite skips above are held to. The suite's own standards doc,
' e2e/go-testing-standards.md, is where the rule is stated for the people
' writing these tests; this block is why it holds.
'
' The sweep narrows with it: `mise run test-e2e` drops
' e2e/docker-detached-smoke.sh and defs/sidecars/mitmproxy/test.sh, and the
' Layer-3 egress integration task (`test-go-integration`) stays outside it —
' Layer 3 is where the egress topology is still exercised, deliberately, by a
' task nobody runs on the PR path.
'
' ── AND SO PROVEO_SBX=off IS BANNED, WHICH IT ALREADY WAS ──────────────────
' The ban predates the rule and is now a consequence of it. No e2e test may pin
' the backend off. The pin was introduced so a probe could inspect the docker
' rendering — the "-egress" container, the broker.env bound into it, a mount
' table — without waiting out a timeout on a host where sbx takes the run. It
' worked, and that was the problem: on a host with sbx installed, the backend
' the OPERATOR gets was the one rendering nothing asserted. The suite certified
' a shape the machine does not run.
'
' The rule above answers that by removing the reason to reach for the pin at
' all. Where a case survives because its subject is not the rendering, two
' techniques still apply, and they are now refinements rather than a ladder:
'   1. Assert the BEHAVIOUR, not the rendering. "git history is writable from
'      inside the harness" is a claim about the harness and holds on sbx;
'      "tracked.txt from the host mount is committable" holds only under
'      docker, because sbx clones committed history and the untracked file
'      never crosses. The second is a docker fact wearing a git claim —
'      rewrite the probe to create its own file and the claim is about the
'      harness again.
'   2. BRANCH on what the run resolved, the way assertPlanIsolation branches on
'      isSbxArgv. A plan scraper that finds no `docker run` has scraped a
'      sandbox plan; read that plan instead of failing. Note what the rule did
'      to that branch: `--print` renders whichever backend resolves, so the
'      DOCKER arm now fires only on a host that cannot run sbx. It is
'      kept because that host is real, not because the arm is exercised here.
' The third technique this list used to carry — skip on the other rendering
' where a mechanism exists in one only — is not a technique any more. It is
' the default, and the governing rule states it.
'
' Where the provider must be detected from a workspace .env alone, the test unsets
' every provider Detect variable but PRESERVES infrastructure ones such as
' DOCKER_HOST, which may point at a remote daemon.
'
' ── WHAT THE CREDENTIAL SUITE ASSERTS, AND OVER WHAT VALUES ─────────────────
' Every value is random per run (`tst_` + 24 random bytes, hex), so no real secret
' is involved and a leak is unambiguous; a mismatch is reported as sha256 prefix +
' length, never by printing the key. Two complementary halves, and the sbx-only
' rule above cut between them rather than through either:
'   * isolation (every agent, deterministic) — one `--print` subtest per agent. The
'     dry-run builds the agent's docker command through the exact same env code path
'     as a real run, so it is faithful and needs no containers. STILL RUNS: its
'     subject is the env code path, not the rendering that consumes it.
'   * egress integrity (live) — one firewall run whose broker.env, bind-mounted into
'     the egress-proxy, must receive each key byte-for-byte. broker.env is
'     agent-independent, so one agent proves the path. NOW SKIPPED: broker.env and
'     the egress-proxy are the docker rendering, and nothing in an sbx run
'     produces either. The probe is kept because what it measures is still worth
'     measuring the day anyone asks the question again.
' claudecode drives the live half, not cursor: cursor declares capabilities
' egress:[open] credentials:[forward], so it has no MITM and therefore no broker at
' all. claudecode declares providers:[anthropic], which narrows the detected set to
' one — and that is precisely what ARMS the broker; with every key detected the
' broker refuses to pin and there is no broker.env to inspect.
' cursor's exception is asserted separately and STILL RUNS, because it reads a
' refusal on the command surface through `--print` rather than a rendering. It is
' declared capabilities rather than a target-name special case: the default
' forwards the REAL key as a bare
' `-e CURSOR_API_KEY`, with no sentinel and no --internal network, and an explicitly
' requested unsupported axis is REFUSED naming what IS allowed ("does not support
' --egress-mode allowlist (allowed: open)") — not silently rewritten, and no longer
' a warn-and-continue that hands over a dead sentinel.
' The project-.env variant proves the same boundary from the other end: a key that
' exists ONLY in the workspace .env reaches the egress sidecar byte-for-byte while
' the agent sees the sentinel and an EMPTY /app/.env. It ran against a lightweight
' probe image (ubuntu:24.04) so the credential path did not depend on any agent
' CLI's release or install state. NOW SKIPPED with the live half, and for the same
' reason — the egress sidecar it reads is the docker rendering.
'
' ── WHY SOME LAYER-4 CASES HAVE NO CONTAINER AT ALL ─────────────────────────
' `proveo init --print` is the one mode that must be safe on any host: it resolves
' the plan and reports this machine's verdict without downloading, installing,
' signing in, or writing anything. Running it against the REAL host is the only way
' to catch a plan that resolves fine in a table test but names a path or a distro
' that does not exist here — and the assertion is symmetric, checking both that the
' output names the pinned release / the asset / the host triple AND that the prefix
' was never created and nothing was left in the working directory. The provenance
' case is Layer 4 for the same reason: the digest is fetched from the LIVE release
' at install time, so a bumped Release with a moved subject name would only fail on
' a host nobody is watching. Details of what init plans are in
' _spec/cmd/proveo/init-sbx-bootstrap.puml.
'
' ── THE INVOCATIONS, AS THE SUITES THEMSELVES RECORD THEM ───────────────────
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run CredentialForwardingIntegrity -v
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run ProjectDotEnvAtEgressLayer -v -timeout 10m
'   [PROVEO_TEST_LOCAL_MODEL=gemma4] \
'     go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run PromptfulE2E -v -timeout 360s
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run InitPrint -v
'   go test -tags=e2e ./e2e/ -run InitProvenance -v
'   go test -tags=integration ./internal/egress/ (+ PROVEO_EGRESS_INTEGRATION=1)

component "**1. Unit**\ninternal/*/*_test.go — no build tags\nGo only · deterministic\n//always//" as L1 <<app>>
component "**2. Contract**\nGo builders + goldens\nDockerRunArgs · BuildPlan · Detect · writeBrokerEnv\n//always, no Docker//" as L2 <<app>>
component "**3. Infra integration**\ninternal/egress ""//go:build integration//""\nreal containers, no model\n//-tags=integration + PROVEO_EGRESS_INTEGRATION=1//" as L3 <<async>>
component "**4. Agent E2E (promptful)**\ne2e ""//go:build e2e//"" via internal/tmux\nnon-deterministic — asserts side effects\n//-tags=e2e · skips per missing prerequisite//" as L4 <<async>>

database "cov/unit\ngo tool covdata merge → percent" as COV <<db>>

L1 ARROW_MAIN L2
L2 ARROW_MAIN L3
L3 ARROW_MAIN L4
L1 ARROW_QUEUE COV
L2 ARROW_QUEUE COV

note right of L1
  Host orchestration under test is **Go**
  (""cmd/proveo"", ""internal/{runner,workspace,egress,
  sbx,gitidentity,manifest}""). Fat bash runners and the
  ""defs/lib/egress.sh"" lifecycle are retired.
end note

note right of L2
  **Go-first contracts:** execute the builders and diff
  the argv/plan, rather than grepping source for literal
  strings. A grep contract survives the deletion of the
  thing it claimed to test — it passes while testing
  nothing. Bash remains only for image smoke suites.
end note

note right of L4
  **sbx is the runtime under test.** Two backends
  render one launch and only one is a subject, so
  a case reading the docker rendering skips and
  names itself; a restated image tag still stops
  matching the day the tag policy moves.
end note

note as SBXONLY
  **Scope, not a defect.** The mitmproxy sidecar
  and the docker fallback still ship; no assertion
  here stands behind them, because a rendering the
  operator never receives cannot certify the
  runtime the operator does receive.
end note

note as SKIPNOTDELETE
  **Skipped preserves the question; deleted
  discards it.** Each retired probe keeps the
  shape it measured and announces its subject in
  the skip, so a scope decision reads as a
  decision rather than an unexplained gap.
end note

note right of L3
  Topology is owned (""BuildPlan"" + ""ExecRunner""), so do
  **not** add testcontainers-go for Squid/proxy. Apply
  its //practices// instead: wait on the CA file / HTTP
  ready (never sleep-only), ""t.Cleanup"" teardown, env
  gate + ""docker"" LookPath skip.
end note

note right of L4
  **Two lanes, keyless.** ""e2e_test.go"" runs
  opencode on a local Ollama model and
  ""hello_world_test.go"" runs all three targets
  on one, supplying no provider credential.
  Both assert side effects, not the model's prose.
end note

note bottom of COV
  Default ""go test ./..."" never compiles Layer 3/4 — the
  PR path stays fast and keyless. No hard coverage gate
  until a baseline exists; CI publishes the percent as an
  artifact.

  **Deferred (Stage 0b):** ""proveo/egress-proxy:cover""
  from ""go build -cover"" + a bind-mounted ""GOCOVERDIR"",
  so Layer 3 reports statement coverage from inside the
  container. Topology coverage does not require it.
end note

